==Controversies==
===GRABHER licence plate===
In 2017, Lorne Grabher of Nova Scotia had his custom licence plate revoked. The plate displayed his last name 'GRABHER', which he had purchased as a gift for his father in the 1990s. He had received a letter saying his plate would be cancelled as some had found his licence plate to be misogynistic and offensive.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Colbert |first1=Yvonne |title=Too rude for the road — government yanks man's last name from licence plate |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/nova-scotia/license-plate-government-regulations-removal-last-name-1.4037912 |website=CBC |publisher=CBC News |access-date=5 August 2022}}</ref> Lorne attempted to keep his plate by fighting back in court, claiming it violated his right to freedom of expression. However, in August 2021 the Nova Scotia Court of Appeals ruled his constitutional rights had not been violated. They concluded the plate could not be used as it could be seen as offensive without the correct context.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Rhodes |first1=Blair |title=Nova Scotia man loses appeal in 'Grabher' licence plate dispute |url=https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/nova-scotia/nova-scotia-lorn-grabher-license-plate-loses-appeal-1.6151452 |website=CBC |publisher=CBC News |access-date=5 August 2022}}</ref> In 2022, the Supreme Court of Canada declined to hear an appeal of his case.<ref>{{cite web |title=Supreme Court won't hear appeal of Nova Scotia man's 'Grabher' licence plate case |url=https://globalnews.ca/news/8689904/grabher-supreme-court-appeal-licence-plate/ |website=Global News |publisher=The Canadian Press |access-date=5 August 2022}}</ref>

